---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import HowManyItemsFitCalculator from '../../components/HowManyItemsFitCalculator';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/box-utilization-tool.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Many Items Fit in a Box? Grid Calculator — Packmetry"
  description="Estimate how many identical rectangular items fit in one box using a uniform 3D grid and permitted rotations. See the limits of a grid estimate."
>
  <main class="pm-utilization-page">
    <div class="pm-utilization-shell">
      <header class="pm-utilization-hero">
        <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Identical-item grid planning</p>

        <h1>
          How many identical items
          fit in your box?
        </h1>

        <p>
          Enter one item and the carton&apos;s internal dimensions.
          Packmetry evaluates uniform, axis-aligned grids for each
          allowed item orientation and shows the best grid count found.
          This is not a claim of global maximum packing capacity.
        </p>
      </header>

      <HowManyItemsFitCalculator client:load />

      <div class="pm-utilization-copy">
        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Method</p>
          <h2>Count whole items along each carton axis.</h2>
          <p>
            For one allowed orientation, grid capacity is
            floor(internal length / item length) ×
            floor(internal width / item width) ×
            floor(internal height / item height).
            The calculator compares the permitted orientations and
            keeps the largest uniform-grid count.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Worked example</p>
          <h2>20 × 20 × 20 cm box, 5 × 5 × 5 cm items.</h2>
          <p>
            Four items line up along each of the three axes:
            4 × 4 × 4 = <strong>64 items</strong> in a uniform grid.
            Each item fits wholly inside the specified internal cavity.
            This simple equal-cube example yields 100% grid-volume
            utilization before packing allowances.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">What the answer means</p>
          <h2>Grid fit is not guaranteed maximum fit.</h2>
          <p>
            The count describes a constructible pattern with the same
            item orientation throughout the grid. Other arrangements,
            including mixed orientations, may fit more items. A
            requested quantity above this grid count therefore does
            not prove the quantity is physically impossible to pack.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Limitations</p>
          <h2>No invisible clearance or handling promises.</h2>
          <p>
            Geometry uses internal carton dimensions only. The
            calculator does not add padding or spacing allowances,
            model fragility, stackability, weight limits or carton
            inventory. Use actual internal dimensions after any
            protective clearance needed for your product.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Compare estimates</p>
          <h2>Volume-only estimates answer a different question.</h2>
          <p>
            The <a href="/tools/box-utilization-calculator/">
              box utilization calculator
            </a> compares requested item volume with carton volume,
            but cannot establish a layout. This tool evaluates an
            actual uniform grid, though it still does not run the
            independent packing verifier.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <p class="pm-tool-kicker">Full packing plan</p>
          <h2>Need more than a single uniform grid?</h2>
          <p>
            Run the <a href="/personal/">Personal workspace</a>
            or <a href="/business/">Business workspace</a> to
            generate packing candidates and verify real item
            placements. Read the
            <a href="/methodology/packing-algorithm/">
              packing methodology
            </a> to understand the solver and its limitations.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
